   MA, FEB, 1998},
Organization = {Eastern Psychol Assoc},
Abstract = {Skin color in relation to perceived attractiveness, personality ratings,
   and perceived life success of African Americans was investigated in a 2
   (sex of participant) x 2 (skin color of stimulus person) x 2 (sex of
   stimulus pet-soil) design. Based oil prior research, Skin Color x Sex of
   Stimulus Person and Sex of Participant x Skin Color interactions were
   hypothesized. Lighter skinned African American women and dark-skinned
   African American men were expected to receive higher attractiveness,
   personality, and life success ratings from White participants. In
   addition, women were expected to rate dark-skinned individuals higher
   The results obtained supported the hypotheses for perceived life success
   only. African American women with fair skin were rated higher than
   fair-skinned African American men oil intelligence and parenting skills.
   In addition, women rated dark-skinned individuals higher than men did
   oil intelligence. Women also rated the dark-skinned individuals higher
   than fair-skinned individuals on enthusiasm. Findings are discussed in
   terms of marketplace theory.},
